---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Cascadia Nature Tokens: When to Spend and When to Hoard"
  description="Each pinecone is a point — but spending one at the right moment is worth far more."
  game="cascadia"
  gameTitle="Cascadia"
  category="Strategy"
  product="cascadia-pinecone-tokens"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Nature tokens are the only part of Cascadia where you get to break the rules,
    and they're the clearest divider between players who've played five games and
    players who've played fifty. New players hoard them for the single point.
    Experienced players spend nearly all of them.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="A nature token is worth one point if you keep it. Spent at the right moment it's routinely worth five to ten. Hoard only when the game is nearly over or the display is already giving you what you need." />

  <h2>What They Actually Do</h2>

  <p>
    You earn a nature token by placing a wildlife token on a
    <strong>keystone tile</strong> — a tile with only one wildlife symbol on it.
    You can then spend one, at any point on your turn, to do one of two things:
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-2">🔀 Break the pairing</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Take <em>any</em> tile from the display with <em>any</em> token from the display. You're no longer restricted to the four fixed pairs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-2">🗑️ Cull the wildlife</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Discard any number of tokens from the display and draw replacements. Useful when nothing on offer helps you — or helps an opponent too much.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="You can do this at any point in your turn">
    Including after seeing the refill. There's no "declare first" restriction, so
    take a moment before committing — the token is still available after you've
    thought about it.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Maths of Spending</h2>

  <p>
    Ask one question: <strong>does spending this token gain me more than one point?</strong>
    The answer is yes far more often than beginners assume.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="What a well-timed token is worth">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Situation</th>
          <th>Typical gain</th>
          <th>Spend?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Completes a long elk line</td>
          <td>5–10 points</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">Always</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Gets the tile that connects two habitat corridors</td>
          <td>3–8 points</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">Always</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Wins you a largest-corridor bonus</td>
          <td>Bonus + tiebreak</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">Yes</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Avoids discarding an animal you needed</td>
          <td>2–5 points</td>
          <td><strong class="text-emerald-700">Usually</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Marginally better tile than the paired one</td>
          <td>0–1 points</td>
          <td><strong class="text-red-700">No</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Final turn, nothing transformative available</td>
          <td>0 points</td>
          <td><strong class="text-red-700">Keep it</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Earning More Than You Spend</h2>

  <p>
    Keystone tiles are the engine. A tile with a single wildlife symbol is
    restrictive — only one animal can ever live there — but it pays you a token
    when you use it correctly.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prioritise keystones you can fill soon.</strong> A keystone for an animal you're not collecting is just a restrictive tile.</li>
    <li><strong>Keystones early are worth more than keystones late.</strong> A token earned on turn three can be spent four or five times' worth of value across the game; one earned on turn nineteen is a single point.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't distort your landscape for them.</strong> A badly placed keystone costs you habitat continuity, which is worth more than the token.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The rule of thumb that works">
    Spend freely in the first two-thirds of the game, then stop. Early tokens
    compound — they fix your engine while there are still turns left to benefit.
    In the last five or six turns, a token in hand is often genuinely the better
    point.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Culling Play Nobody Uses</h2>

  <p>
    Culling gets forgotten because it feels like a wasted turn, but it has a
    defensive use that matters at three and four players: if the display contains
    the exact tile the player after you needs, you can make it disappear.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use it sparingly. Cascadia is a gentle game and table-wide grudges cost more
    than the points. But when someone is one tile from a huge elk line and you're
    within a few points of them, it's entirely fair game.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Watch for free culls">
    If three of the four display tokens match, you may cull them for free at the
    start of your turn — no nature token needed. Players miss this constantly and
    pay a pinecone for something the rules were giving away.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Are They Worth Chasing?</h2>

  <p>
    Not on their own. A player who builds their whole game around collecting
    keystone tiles ends up with a fragmented landscape and mediocre corridors.
    Nature tokens are a lubricant, not a strategy — they make your actual plan
    work more smoothly.
  </p>

  <p>
    That said, in a close finish the pile of unspent pinecones is very often the
    margin, which is a nice piece of design: the resource that helps you play well
    is also the resource that scores.
  </p>
</Article>
